{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Kb43DjYMddU2knyfJmSMYw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Rubric — Binary Search\n2\t\n3\tThe submission is an explanation of binary search on a sorted list of integers.\n4\tIt is scored on five criteria. Full credit means the student explains the\n5\tcriterion clearly enough that a peer could implement it correctly.\n6\t\n7\t1. **Precondition** — the input list must be sorted. The student names it.\n8\t2. **Loop invariant** — at each step, the target (if present) is inside the\n9\t   half-open interval `[lo, hi)` (or an equivalent statement). The student\n10\t   states an invariant, not just \"we shrink the range\".\n11\t3. **Midpoint arithmetic** — the midpoint is computed as `lo + (hi - lo) // 2`,\n12\t   NOT `(lo + hi) // 2`, to avoid integer overflow on large lists. The student\n13\t   names the overflow reason, or uses the safe form and says it is safe.\n14\t4. **Termination** — the loop terminates when `lo == hi`, with the target found\n15\t   or absent. The student states the termination condition explicitly.\n16\t5. **Complexity** — O(log n) time, O(1) extra space. The student names both.\n17\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019wLz9AMuJyDRjgErvQQa9f","session_id":"94b04c7b-bff3-4668-8bbe-c03cbc284631","uuid":"420262d9-6e4b-457a-a340-8ed9ba153aa4","timestamp":"2026-09-10T02:41:23.374Z","subagent_type":"pattern-analyzer","task_description":"Analyze binary-search misconceptions"}
