{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YLeE4WpjhtC2LU5e7xwgEC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Binary search — Tao\n2\t\n3\tBinary search needs the list to be sorted first — that is the precondition.\n4\tOn each step you compare the middle element to the target and eliminate the\n5\thalf that cannot contain it. The invariant is that the target (if present)\n6\tlives in `[lo, hi)` at every step; the loop terminates when `lo == hi`.\n7\t\n8\tI use `mid = lo + (hi - lo) // 2`. That avoids the overflow that\n9\t`(lo + hi) // 2` can hit for very large lists.\n10\t\n11\tTime is O(log n), extra space is O(1).\n12\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019wLz9AMuJyDRjgErvQQa9f","session_id":"94b04c7b-bff3-4668-8bbe-c03cbc284631","uuid":"7cef2d3c-9399-4c90-ac2b-c3fed995d969","timestamp":"2026-09-10T02:41:30.015Z","subagent_type":"pattern-analyzer","task_description":"Analyze binary-search misconceptions"}
